WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.385
The unsafe keyword marks code where the
programmer must uphold rules the compiler

2
00:00:04.465 --> 00:00:05.422
cannot fully check.

3
00:00:05.502 --> 00:00:07.303
Unsafe Rust is not automatically bad,

4
00:00:07.383 --> 00:00:11.437
and some low-level libraries,
performance-sensitive code, platform integration,

5
00:00:11.517 --> 00:00:14.050
and interoperability work legitimately
require it.

6
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.875
But unsafe changes the review model.

7
00:00:15.955 --> 00:00:17.419
It creates a trusted boundary.

8
00:00:17.499 --> 00:00:21.215
Defensive Rust keeps unsafe code rare,
justified, isolated, and documented.

9
00:00:21.295 --> 00:00:23.941
Reviewers should be able to answer why
unsafe is needed,

10
00:00:24.021 --> 00:00:26.498
what invariants must hold, what callers
may assume,

11
00:00:26.578 --> 00:00:30.350
and how the code prevents unsafe
assumptions from leaking across the rest of the

12
00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.106
application.

13
00:00:31.186 --> 00:00:34.226
The smaller the unsafe surface, the easier
that review becomes.

14
00:00:34.306 --> 00:00:36.332
Safe wrappers are often the right pattern.

15
00:00:36.412 --> 00:00:40.297
Unsafe internals can sometimes expose a
safe public API when the wrapper enforces

16
00:00:40.377 --> 00:00:41.616
the required invariants.

17
00:00:41.696 --> 00:00:45.299
That wrapper should define what inputs are
valid, who owns returned values,

18
00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:47.743
what lifetimes mean, how errors are
represented,

19
00:00:47.823 --> 00:00:49.681
and whether concurrent use is allowed.

20
00:00:49.761 --> 00:00:53.533
Foreign function interfaces require
special attention because Rust's normal

21
00:00:53.613 --> 00:00:56.203
guarantees may not apply across language
boundaries.

22
00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:59.154
Review should cover pointer validity,
ownership transfer,

23
00:00:59.234 --> 00:01:02.499
allocation and deallocation
responsibility, thread assumptions,

24
00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:05.169
error conventions, data layout, and
representation.

25
00:01:05.249 --> 00:01:09.134
These details should be written down, not
carried as unwritten tribal knowledge.

26
00:01:09.214 --> 00:01:12.197
Boundary tests and documentation are part
of the safety story.

27
00:01:12.277 --> 00:01:14.755
The goal is not to forbid unsafe code
categorically.

28
00:01:14.835 --> 00:01:18.156
The goal is to keep trusted assumptions
small, explicit, reviewable,

29
00:01:18.236 --> 00:01:22.064
and exercised by tests so safe Rust
remains the default experience for the rest of

30
00:01:22.144 --> 00:01:22.820
the codebase.
